-- title:
--   Descendant blocks are intervals
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix levels $i\le j$ of an ordered tree and a node $v$ of level $i$.
--
--   1. **First and last descendants.** The least and greatest descendants of $v$ at level $j$.
--   2. **Endpoints.** $\mathrm{lo}(v)$ is the index of the first descendant and $\mathrm{hi}(v)$ is one more than the index of the last. Since ancestor maps are monotone and surjective, the descendants of $v$ are exactly the leaves with
--   $$
--   \mathrm{lo}(v)\le s<\mathrm{hi}(v),
--   $$
--   a nonempty interval whose upper endpoint may equal the size of level $j$.
